An interactive map website that highlights important places. Each marker opens a panel with photos and a note telling the story of what happened there. It’s a gift designed to be explored like a museum.

```
lovellaWebPage/
│
├── frontend/                  # Public-facing website: catalog, cart, checkout, user accounts
│   ├── public/                 # Fixed, rarely-changing site assets referenced by direct path (favicon, logo)
│   ├── js/
│   │   ├── features/            # Shared business logic used across multiple pages (cart state, auth/session handling) — not tied to any single page
│   │   └── pages/                # Page-specific scripts that render each view and call into features/ and api.js
│   ├── styles/                 # CSS per page, plus a main.css holding global variables and base styles
│   ├── assets/                 # Growing content assets: UI icons and any static site imagery (not product photos — those live on the backend, see below)
│   │
│   └── admin/                  # Nested admin panel for managing the store, restricted to admin users
│       ├── js/                  # Admin-specific page scripts (product forms, order tables), reusing frontend/js/features/ and api.js
│       └── styles/              # Admin-specific layout and page styles (sidebar, dashboard, tables)
│
├── backend/                   # FastAPI application handling data, auth, and business logic
│   ├── app/
│   │   ├── main.py               # Entry point, mounts routers and serves static files
│   │   ├── api/                  # Route definitions grouped by resource (products, cart, orders, payments, auth, admin)
│   │   ├── models/               # Database schema definitions (SQLAlchemy)
│   │   ├── schemas/              # Request/response validation (Pydantic)
│   │   ├── services/             # Business logic separated from routes (totals, stock checks, etc.)
│   │   ├── db/                   # Database connection and migrations
│   │   ├── core/                 # Security, JWT handling, and app configuration
│   │   └── static/
│   │       └── products/          # Actual product image files, uploaded via the admin panel; the DB only stores each product's image path/URL, not the file itself
│   └── tests/                  # Automated tests for backend logic and endpoints
│
├── docs/                      # Project documentation
└── .github/                   # CI/CD workflows for deployment and testing
```

This is a structure I propose for the project, vanilla JavaScript. Any of these details could be modified in the future.